WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.900
Conditional Access and MFA can affect how users and workloads access protected resources.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:19.880
A .NET application may encounter claims challenges when a resource requires additional policy satisfaction, such as multi-factor authentication, device state, location policy, session control, or other tenant-defined requirements.

3
00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:27.840
Web apps, web APIs, on-behalf-of flows, and Microsoft Graph calls can be affected differently by tenant policies.

4
00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:35.540
A user may sign in to the application successfully and still encounter a policy requirement when the application calls a downstream API.

5
00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:43.640
Developers should distinguish authentication failures, authorization failures, consent problems, and Conditional Access challenges.

6
00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:50.640
Continuous Access Evaluation and session controls may affect token lifetime and access behavior.

7
00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:54.740
Applications should handle policy-driven failures predictably and safely.

8
00:00:54.840 --> 00:01:03.480
That may involve prompting the user through a supported library flow, returning a clear API response, or recording an operational event for review.

9
00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:09.280
Do not hardcode assumptions that all tenants have the same Conditional Access behavior.

10
00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:18.500
Enterprise customers may have different MFA requirements, device policies, sign-in risk policies, network restrictions, and session controls.

11
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:27.480
A robust .NET application treats tenant policy as part of the integration surface and avoids turning policy variance into confusing failures.
